  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nNotable limitations and hypothesis:\n- Expanding the search space beyond the parent solution with higher resolution may uncover superior constructions\n- Existing optimization falls short of theoretical bounds\n- Manual restart strategies are unlikely to improve the known bound\nProposed approach: \nA theoretically motivated, deterministic construction using piecewise sinusoidal functions with specific harmonics that maximize contrast between neighboring regions while maintaining integral balance and low correlation. This differs from stochastic optimization by:\n1) Careful amplitude/compliance calibration through analytical bounds\n2) Structural design rather than iterative improvement\n3) Guaranteed feasibility at all steps\nCentral idea: \nDesign h(x) to minimize the maximum correlation by segregating high and low states into distinct regions of the circle {x: |x| <= 2}. This combines ideas from Erd\u0151s-Easley threshold phenomena and optimal information retrieval system design\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresentation:\nPiecewise sinusoidal functions defined on[0,2] with n_grid divisions and m_segments empirically determined segments\nVariables:\n- Segment break points s_i (0 <= s1 < s2 < ... < s_m < 2)\n- Phase \u03c6_k ([0,2\u03c0))\n- Amplitude factor a_k (0 < a_k < 1)\n- Base value b_k (0.1 to 0.9)\nConstraints:\nIntegral from 0 to 2 of h(x) dx = 1\n0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\nSmoothness at break points (Lipschitz continuity with parameter L_max)\nObjective function:\nC_5\u207b = min_over_h max_k \u222b[0,2] h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\nvia discrete approximation with grid spacing dx=2/n_grid\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Initialization\nn_grid starting from 720 (doubling parent grid)\nGenerate m_segments= empirical_max_segments(n_grid) = min(100, n_grid//8)\nDistribute break points uniformly: s_i = 2*i/m_segments (but adaptive later)\n\nb) Segment parameterization\nEach segment j has:\n- Position [s_j, s_{j+1}) covered by a function:\n   h_j(x) = base + amplitude * sin(2\u03c0 * frequency(x - s_j) + \u03c6)\nwhere frequency between 0.5 and 1.5 in the empirical range\n\nc) Feasibility and optimization\nExact integral enforcement by:\n  Total_integral = sum_j int_base + sum_j amplitude_j * Fourier_integral(frequency, [s_j, s_{j+1}])\n\nd) Constraint handling\nMaintain h(x) between min_base and max_base by scaling\nAdjust amplitudes to maintain integral via Jacobian-based least squares\n\ne) Optimization phase\n1) Coarse search: centroidal Voronoi to identify potential amplitudes\n2) Fine search: deterministic gradient-based optimization ensuring integral constraints\n3) Hill climbing: Simulated annealing with state representation in Fourier domain\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault parameters:\nbase_values: [0.2 to 0.8] divided log-uniformly\namplitude_factors: [0.1, 0.9] with high standard deviation initially\nfrequency_domain: [0.5, 1.5] with quarter-wave harmonics prioritized\nLipschitz constant: L_max = \u03c0 * dx (initial)\n\nInitial configuration:\nn_segments = 3 (hard-coded minimization)\nbase1=0.2, amplitude1=0.6, (frequency=1.0, \u03c6=0)\nbase2=0.7, amplitude2=0.6, (frequency=1.0, \u03c6=\u03c0/2)\n\nProgression rule:\nTriple segment resolution if width-based Lipschitz indicator is violated\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime allocation:\n- Coarse search: 20% of total budget\n- Fine search: 60%\n- Hill climbing: 20%\nOptimizations:\n- Dynamic resolution adjustment (n_grid up to 1440)\n- Early termination if overlap < 0.35\n- Fallback to parent solution at each improvement plateau\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nAfter optimization:\n- Verify 0 <= h(x) <=1 with tight tolerances\n- Compute c5_bound = dx * max_k np.correlate(h, 1-h, 'full').max()\n- Maintain n_grid history for resolution relevance\nFallback strategy:\nIf optimization exceeds timeout/ineffectual, return the best verified candidate",
